{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788303483,"nonce":"FEOg7MSXvbFVCLlB543QCANLHRk=","message":"RULED BY EVAN, 2026-09-01: **the default bound on an `until` advance is ONE IN-GAME DAY (60,000 ticks)** — and his reasoning reframes what the number is for, so it is recorded here rather than just the value.\n\n\u003e \"a full day without doing anything while you're fully set is pretty typical. Lots of things the colony does itself day to day and ideally if something bad happens, you'll be woken up, you won't have to check.\"\n\n## What that changes about the fix\n\nI proposed 60,000 as a **cap on a wedge** — short enough that a stuck run does not eat a day, long enough not to chop legitimate waits (session 18's room build was 3,180 ticks). Evan accepted the number and corrected the framing:\n\n**A quiet day is the NORMAL case, not the pathological one.** A set-up colony runs itself; the agent is supposed to be able to say \"advance\" and go away. So the default bound is not a safety net bolted onto an error path — it is the natural idle period of the play loop, and an advance that runs a full day and returns is the system working.\n\n**That makes the HALTS the wake-up mechanism, and it raises their status.** \"Ideally if something bad happens, you'll be woken up, you won't have to check\" is a design statement about the whole loop: the agent should not be polling. Which means:\n\n- `722c951`'s own-faction casualty halt is not a guard rail, it is the primary interrupt. It is doing the job the default bound is deliberately NOT doing.\n- The value of the bound is therefore mostly \"eventually return control even when nothing interesting happened\", not \"catch a bug\".\n- And it puts weight on a question this issue does not have to answer but the next round should: **what else should wake the agent?** A raid letter, a breakdown, a mood collapse, a food cliff. If a day of quiet is the intended idle unit, the halt set has to be good enough that a day of quiet is genuinely safe to sleep through. Worth its own issue rather than smuggled in here.\n\n## The fix, as ruled\n\n1. **Default `timeout_ticks` to 60,000** when an `until` advance supplies none. The envelope must say the default was APPLIED rather than requested, so a caller can tell its own bound from ours.\n2. **Refuse at arm time** when `true_when_armed` is true, `edge` is true, and no `timeout_ticks` was given — the halt is unreachable by construction and the mod already computes and publishes that fact. The refusal must be derived from `true_when_armed`, not from a second computation of the same thing, so the field and the enforcement cannot disagree.\n3. **The refusal should NAME `edge:false`.** It already ships as the \"assert now\" reading, and a caller who wrote `time.tick \u003e= now + N` and lost the race almost always meant \"stop as soon as this holds\". Handing them the correct call is better than rejecting theirs.\n\nBoth 1 and 2 are wanted, and the reason is the race documented in the comment above: refusal alone makes the same call fail or not depending on protocol latency, which is a flaky refusal rather than a fix. With a default bound the lost race is benign — no edge comes, the advance ends at the bound having advanced roughly what the caller wanted.\n\n`~~publish armed_true~~` remains withdrawn: it ships as `true_when_armed`.","files":null}]}